---
// The pitfalls list of a guide or of the reference page: one item per pitfall, a bold title above its labeled lines.
// Write a Markdown list inside it; each item is a bold title and one nested item per label, in this order: *You write*,
// *What happens:* and *Write instead:*. A compiler error's item has no *What happens:*, as its title names the error.
// A Proof or LeakCard at the end of a line stays at the end of that line. No props.
// Usage:
//   <Pitfalls>
//
//   - **Keeping the owner and the span apart.**
//     - *You write* a method that returns the `Span<byte>` and lets the owner go out of scope.
//     - *What happens:* the owner is never disposed, so the buffer is never zeroed.
//     - *Write instead:* keep the owner in the scope that uses the span, and dispose it there.
//
//   </Pitfalls>
---

<div class="pitfalls">
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  .pitfalls > :global(ul) {
    display: grid;
    /* minmax(0, …): an item never widens past the column, whatever unbreakable text it holds. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--nc-space-5);
    margin: var(--nc-space-4) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .pitfalls > :global(ul > li) { margin: 0; }
  .pitfalls > :global(ul > li > ul) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--nc-space-2);
    margin: var(--nc-space-2) 0 0;
    padding-inline-start: var(--nc-space-4);
    border-inline-start: var(--nc-border-hair) solid var(--nc-color-border);
    list-style: none;
  }
  .pitfalls > :global(ul > li > ul > li) { margin: 0; }
  .pitfalls :global(li > p) { margin: 0; }
  .pitfalls > :global(ul > li > ul > li > em:first-child),
  .pitfalls > :global(ul > li > ul > li > p:first-child > em:first-child) {
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--nc-color-text-secondary);
  }
</style>
